/** 

@page benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_single_black_hole Single Schwarzschild black hole

<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5e/BH_LMC.png" />

<!-- Add this one for href links to subitems -->
@tableofcontents



Benchmark which solves the single black hole (schwarzschild black hole) described in a ExaGRyPE simulation. 
While the output is not particularly exciting (the simulation should yield a 
stationary solution after a while), it is numerically challenging as we have
sophisticated boundary condition effects, a singularity in the centre which 
can lead to oscillations, need a scheme with low numerical dissipation, and 
have highly varying spatial resolution needs, i.e. need a fine resolution around
the black hole, while we can keep the mesh coarse far away . 


The benchmark comes along in different flavours:

- with AMR vs a regular mesh;
- using one solver only or a combination of two solvers;
- different multicore tasking strategies.


<div style="background-color: #cfc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
  Prior to benchmarking this setup, we recommend that you should ensure
  that @ref benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_kernel_benchmarks and the 
  @ref benchmarks_exahype2_ccz4_gauge_wave complete successfully. 
  One assesses the computational efficiency for various kernel variants over
  different platforms, and the other validates the numerical accuracy of the 
  overall solver assembly (convergence orders).
</div>


# Setting up the experiments

## Build executable

<div style="background-color: #ccf ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
  This benchmark requires a third-party tool: You will need the GNU Scientific Library (GSL) installed on your system. 
</div>


For this single black hole benchmark, the following Peano toolboxes are mandatory:
  
  - blockstructured (```--enable-blockstructured```)
  - exahype2 (```--enable-exahype2```)
  
In theory, the benchmark passes without parallelisation. In practice, it is so 
computationally demanding that we recommend to use multithreading and MPI. Due 
to this, you also need

   - load balancing (```--enable-load-balancing```)

GPU support is, as always, optional. Other advanced features such as particle 
tracers are not used. 

Builds always resemble:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export PYTHONPATH=../../../../python:../../../../applications/exahype2/ccz4

python3 performance-studies.py -s fd4-rk1-ps-3 -et 1.0 --plot 0.1 --cell-size 1.8
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


As always, the parameter ```--help``` makes the Python script yield further info,
while the sections below enlist characteristic choices of the parameters.

Depending on your system, you might have to load the GSL module separately
before you build (and run) the application:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
module load gsl
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~




## Clean up old and temporary files

The following statements remove all the glue code and autogenerated C++ files.
There is no risk in deleting them - if you rerun the Python script, they will 
all be reinstantiated.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
rm *.o *.cmake
rm README-*.md
rm Makefile CMakeLists.txt
rm Abstract*
rm -rf celldata facedata globaldata observers tasks vertexdata repositories
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

To get rid of the output files, type in

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
rm *patch-file *vtu *.pvd *.bak *.csv
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

