# Benchmark builds

Benchmarking the single black hole is not easy, as it is a rather complex setup
already with a rather long initialisation and grid construction phase. However,
it is possible to benchmark it once you disable plots, the tracers, and you use
an unreasonable coarse mesh. That is, we disable a lot of key features for the 
benchmarking runs. In general, we apply the following simplifications:


- We reduce the end time drastically to ```-et 1e-2``` via the Python API front-end.
- We omit any output by defaulting the ```--plot 0``` from the Python call to zero which switches off the plotting.


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 performance-studies.py -s fd4-rk1-ps-3 -et 1e-2 --plot 0.0 --cell-size 1.8
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Here are some canonical settings:


Description     | Solver         | Cell size | End time | Patch size | Remarks   | Build command
----------------|----------------|-----------|----------|------------|-----------|---------------
Minimal setup   | Finite Volumes | 5.4       | 1.0      |          6 | Toy problem with 729 cells. Tells you if the code, in principle, works. Should crash at one point suddenly as the eigenvalue becomes zero, which showcases that this setup is too coarse to capture anything that has a physical meaning. | ```python3 performance-studies.py -s fv-6 -et 1.0 --plot 0.0 --cell-size 5.4```
Simplest FV setup for single node workstation (laptop)  | Finite Volumes | 1.8       | 1e-1     |          6 | You cannot see AMR here, but already gives you 19,683 cells. Load balancing should be really good. This is kind of the largest regular mesh setup that I can fit on a local laptop. | ```python3 performance-studies.py -s fv-6 -et 0.1 --plot 0.0 --cell-size 1.8```


## Tasks scheduling and orchestration

For each of these benchmarks, I test them with various schedulers:

- ```--scheduler native``` Picks the native, hardcoded scheduler from Peano. 
  This disables all task fusion and also all GPU offloading.
- ```--scheduler native-piorities``` Constructs an instance of 
  @ref benchmarks::exahype2::ccz4::MulticoreOrchestration as task orchestration. It still disables all the 
  task fusion (and therefore GPU offloading), but now activates features 
  such as 
- ```--scheduler gpu-lazy-evaluation``` Tasks are shipped off to the GPU lazily,
  i.e.~when the task results are requested. Compare to the description
  in @ref tarch::multicore::orchestration::FusionImplementation.
- ```--scheduler gpu-timed-execution``` Tasks are shipped en bloc to the GPU after
  each mesh sweep that produces tasks. Compare to the description
  in @ref tarch::multicore::orchestration::FusionImplementation.
- ```--scheduler gpu-producer-consumer``` Shipped off to the GPU with producer-consumer 
  tasksCompare to the description
  in @ref tarch::multicore::orchestration::FusionImplementation.
- ```--scheduler gpu-eager-consumer-tasks``` Tasks are shipped off to the GPU using
  Peano's eager consumer pattern.Compare to the description
  in @ref tarch::multicore::orchestration::FusionImplementation.
- ```--scheduler --no-tasks``` No tasking is used, i.e. everything that could be mapped onto a task is executed immediately.


If an orchestration with task fusion is picked and you employ a combined FV-FD 
solver, then only the Finite Volume tasks are sent off to the accelerator. 
Details on this behaviour can be found in 
@ref benchmarks::exahype2::ccz4::MulticoreOrchestration.


